1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:09,659
On July 18th, 1969, two days before Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon, a speech was typed in the White House announcing their deaths.

2
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:15,895
Not as a precaution buried in a filing cabinet. It came with a protocol.

3
00:00:15,895 --> 00:00:21,719
Who the President would call. When NASA would cut the radio. What the priest would say.

4
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:26,224
Because everyone involved knew something the public celebration hid.

5
00:00:26,224 --> 00:00:32,192
If the single engine on the lunar lander failed to light, there was no rescue. None.

6
00:00:33,333 --> 00:00:41,692
Armstrong and Aldrin would be left on the surface, alive and perfectly healthy, waiting for their oxygen to run out.

7
00:00:41,692 --> 00:00:43,782
On live television's greatest triumph.

8
00:00:44,899 --> 00:00:51,010
And here is the part almost nobody knows. On the Moon, that nightmare very nearly began.

9
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:55,975
Because of a broken plastic switch, and a backpack that bumped a wall.

10
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:01,135
This is the full story of the plan for losing Apollo 11.

11
00:01:01,135 --> 00:01:07,861
The odds they hid, the memo they wrote, the switch that snapped, and the felt-tip pen that saved the mission.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,846
Chapter one. The odds they never told you.

13
00:01:12,966 --> 00:01:19,627
In public, Apollo 11 was confidence itself. Half a million people lined the Florida coast.

14
00:01:19,627 --> 00:01:24,512
The crew smiled and waved. America was going to the Moon.

15
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:29,562
In private, the men in the capsule were running very different numbers.

16
00:01:29,562 --> 00:01:34,802
Neil Armstrong later said he believed they had a ninety percent chance of coming home alive.

17
00:01:35,933 --> 00:01:40,064
But only a fifty percent chance of actually landing. A coin flip.

18
00:01:40,064 --> 00:01:45,572
That was the commander's own estimate of the thing the whole world had gathered to watch.

19
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:48,679
The insurance industry agreed with him.

20
00:01:48,679 --> 00:01:55,607
No company would write a sane life insurance policy on a man about to ride a Saturn V to another world.

21
00:01:56,733 --> 00:02:00,226
So the crew invented their own life insurance.

22
00:02:00,226 --> 00:02:05,902
In quarantine before launch, they sat and signed hundreds of postal envelopes. Autographs.

23
00:02:07,033 --> 00:02:09,823
The plan was simple and quietly heartbreaking.

24
00:02:09,823 --> 00:02:14,606
If they died on the Moon, their families would sell the signatures.

25
00:02:14,606 --> 00:02:17,795
A dead moonwalker's autograph would pay for college.

26
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:22,652
Those envelopes exist today. Collectors call them insurance covers.

27
00:02:22,652 --> 00:02:28,024
They are the physical proof of how the crew really rated their chances.

28
00:02:29,166 --> 00:02:34,101
NASA's own engineers had spent years studying the ways the mission could fail.

29
00:02:34,101 --> 00:02:37,138
One failure mode stood above all the others.

30
00:02:37,138 --> 00:02:40,555
It had no backup, no workaround, and no survivors.

31
00:02:41,699 --> 00:02:46,983
It lived in the spidery machine bolted to the top of the rocket. The lunar module.

32
00:02:46,983 --> 00:02:50,946
And to understand the death speech, you have to understand this machine.

33
00:02:52,066 --> 00:02:55,775
Chapter two. One engine, no second chances.

34
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:00,485
The lunar module Eagle was two spacecraft stacked together.

35
00:03:00,485 --> 00:03:03,672
A descent stage with legs, for going down.

36
00:03:03,672 --> 00:03:08,054
And an ascent stage, the crew cabin, for coming back up.

37
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:13,410
Getting off the Moon depended on exactly one thing. The ascent engine.

38
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:19,727
A single rocket motor about the size of a washing machine, with thirty five hundred pounds of thrust.

39
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:24,910
There was no backup engine. There was no rescue ship.

40
00:03:24,910 --> 00:03:28,550
There was no second lunar module waiting in orbit.

41
00:03:28,550 --> 00:03:31,785
The engineering philosophy was brutal in its simplicity.

42
00:03:32,933 --> 00:03:36,687
Make the one engine so simple it cannot fail.

43
00:03:36,687 --> 00:03:41,692
No ignition system, because the fuels explode on contact with each other.

44
00:03:41,692 --> 00:03:45,446
No fuel pumps, because pressurized helium pushes the propellant.

45
00:03:46,566 --> 00:03:51,196
Open two valves, and physics does the rest. That was the theory.

46
00:03:51,196 --> 00:03:56,597
But this engine, in this configuration, had never been fired on the Moon before.

47
00:03:57,733 --> 00:04:07,999
It would get exactly one attempt, in temperatures swinging hundreds of degrees, in a vacuum, covered in abrasive lunar dust, after days of vibration and violence.

48
00:04:09,133 --> 00:04:16,596
If it lit, the ascent stage would climb to lunar orbit and meet Michael Collins in the command module.

49
00:04:16,596 --> 00:04:19,739
Eighteen minutes of flight. Routine, if everything worked.

50
00:04:20,866 --> 00:04:28,494
If it did not light, Armstrong and Aldrin were dead men in perfect health, standing in the Sea of Tranquility with the Earth hanging above them.

51
00:04:29,633 --> 00:04:34,854
Mission control could talk to them. Their families could, in principle, say goodbye.

52
00:04:34,854 --> 00:04:39,272
And roughly six hundred million people would be watching it happen.

53
00:04:40,399 --> 00:04:44,865
Somebody in the White House realized the government had no words prepared for that.

54
00:04:44,865 --> 00:04:46,460
His name was William Safire.

55
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:50,926
And what he wrote is one of the most haunting documents in American history.

56
00:04:52,066 --> 00:04:55,148
Chapter three. In event of moon disaster.

57
00:04:56,266 --> 00:04:58,481
William Safire was Nixon's speechwriter.

58
00:04:58,481 --> 00:05:06,454
In July 1969, astronaut Frank Borman, NASA's liaison to the White House, called him with a quiet request.

59
00:05:07,599 --> 00:05:15,294
Borman told him to think about, quote, some alternative posture for the President in the event of mishaps.

60
00:05:15,294 --> 00:05:17,003
Safire didn't immediately understand.

61
00:05:18,133 --> 00:05:24,068
So Borman said it plainly. Like what to do for the widows. Safire understood then.

62
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:28,817
The most likely disaster was not an explosion. It was a stranding.

63
00:05:29,966 --> 00:05:37,817
On July 18th, while Apollo 11 coasted toward the Moon, Safire sent a two page memo to Nixon's chief of staff.

64
00:05:37,817 --> 00:05:40,807
The subject line: In event of moon disaster.

65
00:05:41,933 --> 00:05:44,003
The speech it contained begins like this.

66
00:05:44,003 --> 00:05:51,102
Fate has ordained that the men who went to the moon to explore in peace will stay on the moon to rest in peace.

67
00:05:52,233 --> 00:05:58,154
These brave men, Neil Armstrong and Edwin Aldrin, know that there is no hope for their recovery.

68
00:05:58,154 --> 00:06:02,682
But they also know that there is hope for mankind in their sacrifice.

69
00:06:03,799 --> 00:06:06,404
And it ends with a line writers still study.

70
00:06:06,404 --> 00:06:14,797
For every human being who looks up at the moon in the nights to come will know that there is some corner of another world that is forever mankind.

71
00:06:15,933 --> 00:06:17,500
Notice what the speech does.

72
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:23,456
It is written in the present tense, for men who would still be alive when the President read it.

73
00:06:23,456 --> 00:06:25,024
A eulogy for the living.

74
00:06:26,166 --> 00:06:31,234
The memo also laid out the protocol around the speech, and this is the part that stays with you.

75
00:06:32,366 --> 00:06:38,218
First. Before the statement, the President would telephone each of the widows to be.

76
00:06:38,218 --> 00:06:44,487
That is the memo's actual phrase. Widows to be. Their husbands would still be breathing.

77
00:06:45,633 --> 00:06:45,968
Second.

78
00:06:45,968 --> 00:06:55,690
After the speech, at the point when NASA ends communication with the men — the space agency would deliberately cut the radio link, and leave them to the silence.

79
00:06:56,833 --> 00:06:57,164
Third.

80
00:06:57,164 --> 00:07:04,461
A clergyman would then commend their souls to the deepest of the deep, following the same procedure as a burial at sea.

81
00:07:05,599 --> 00:07:09,381
The Moon would become a grave, and the ceremony already existed.

82
00:07:09,381 --> 00:07:14,193
The memo went into a folder, and everyone hoped it would never come out.

83
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:22,455
The document stayed unknown for thirty years, until an archivist found it in the National Archives in 1999.

84
00:07:22,455 --> 00:07:26,017
When it was published, it stunned even Apollo veterans.

85
00:07:27,166 --> 00:07:29,696
But in July 1969 it was live policy.

86
00:07:29,696 --> 00:07:36,335
And there was one man whose entire role in history would be defined by that memo if it were ever used.

87
00:07:37,466 --> 00:07:40,548
Chapter four. The man who would come home alone.

88
00:07:41,666 --> 00:07:45,410
Michael Collins flew to the Moon and never touched it.

89
00:07:45,410 --> 00:07:51,775
While Armstrong and Aldrin descended, he stayed alone in the command module Columbia, circling sixty miles up.

90
00:07:52,899 --> 00:07:56,599
Every orbit took him behind the Moon, where the radio died.

91
00:07:56,599 --> 00:08:03,661
For forty eight minutes at a time, he was cut off from Earth, from his crewmates, from every human being alive.

92
00:08:04,799 --> 00:08:07,758
Mission control called him the loneliest man since Adam.

93
00:08:07,758 --> 00:08:12,688
Collins said the loneliness wasn't what scared him. What scared him was the other thing.

94
00:08:13,833 --> 00:08:15,249
He wrote it down later.

95
00:08:15,249 --> 00:08:20,912
My secret terror for the last six months has been leaving them on the Moon and returning to Earth alone.

96
00:08:22,033 --> 00:08:28,147
If the ascent engine failed, Collins had strict orders. He could not rescue them.

97
00:08:28,147 --> 00:08:32,952
Columbia could not land. There was nothing to do but leave.

98
00:08:34,099 --> 00:08:36,831
He was blunt about what that would make him.

99
00:08:36,831 --> 00:08:41,384
If they fail to rise from the surface, I am not going to commit suicide.

100
00:08:41,384 --> 00:08:46,847
I am coming home, forthwith. But I will be a marked man for life, and I know it.

101
00:08:47,966 --> 00:08:53,540
Imagine that return. Three weeks of flight, alone, in a ship built for three.

102
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:59,512
A parade nobody would hold. The one man who came back from mankind's greatest tragedy.

103
00:09:00,633 --> 00:09:03,791
Collins kept a small card of emergency procedures.

104
00:09:03,791 --> 00:09:08,923
Eighteen different contingency plans for rescuing the lunar module from a bad orbit.

105
00:09:08,923 --> 00:09:14,844
For a dead engine on the surface, there was no card. There was no page.

106
00:09:15,966 --> 00:09:21,139
That was the machinery of disaster, fully assembled. A speech. A protocol.

107
00:09:21,139 --> 00:09:28,897
A man in orbit rehearsing the worst trip home in history. All of it waiting on one engine.

108
00:09:30,033 --> 00:09:35,284
And before that engine even got its chance, the landing itself almost ended everything.

109
00:09:36,433 --> 00:09:39,750
Chapter five. The rehearsals for disaster.

110
00:09:40,899 --> 00:09:44,041
Apollo 11 didn't walk into that risk blind.

111
00:09:44,041 --> 00:09:48,754
In the eight months before it, NASA flew three full dress rehearsals.

112
00:09:48,754 --> 00:09:52,288
Each one came with its own brush with catastrophe.

113
00:09:53,433 --> 00:09:58,787
December 1968. Apollo 8 carries the first humans ever to leave Earth orbit.

114
00:09:58,787 --> 00:10:05,789
Before launch, commander Frank Borman's own family privately rated his odds of coming home at fifty fifty.

115
00:10:06,933 --> 00:10:13,330
Their engine burn to leave lunar orbit happened behind the Moon, out of radio contact, on Christmas morning.

116
00:10:13,330 --> 00:10:18,662
If it failed, three men would orbit the Moon until their air ran out. Silently.

117
00:10:19,799 --> 00:10:27,192
When the ship emerged from behind the Moon on time, Jim Lovell radioed the words: please be informed, there is a Santa Claus.

118
00:10:28,333 --> 00:10:33,031
It was Borman, remember, who later asked Safire to prepare the disaster speech.

119
00:10:33,031 --> 00:10:39,174
He had done the math on his own mission. He knew exactly what he was asking for.

120
00:10:40,299 --> 00:10:42,080
May 1969. Apollo 10.

121
00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:51,871
Tom Stafford and Gene Cernan fly the lunar module Snoopy to within fifteen kilometers of the surface — everything except the landing.

122
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:03,866
During staging, a switch set in the wrong position sends the lunar module into violent tumbling — flipping end over end above the Moon, gyrating toward gimbal lock.

123
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:11,093
Cernan's exclamation went out on a hot mike to the whole world. Son of a... well.

124
00:11:11,093 --> 00:11:16,807
You can look up the rest. Stafford wrestled the ship back under control in seconds.

125
00:11:17,933 --> 00:11:24,489
Analysts later estimated that a few more seconds of tumbling could have ended with Snoopy in the lunar surface.

126
00:11:24,489 --> 00:11:26,214
Two months before Armstrong's landing.

127
00:11:27,333 --> 00:11:32,631
So when Eagle undocked in July, nobody involved believed the danger was theoretical.

128
00:11:32,631 --> 00:11:37,521
The rehearsals had made one thing clear. The Moon punishes small mistakes.

129
00:11:38,666 --> 00:11:43,812
Chapter six. Twelve minutes, five alarms, twenty five seconds.

130
00:11:44,933 --> 00:11:52,266
July 20th, 1969. Eagle undocks and begins the twelve minute fall to the lunar surface.

131
00:11:52,266 --> 00:11:55,199
Almost immediately, things start going wrong.

132
00:11:56,333 --> 00:12:01,825
Five minutes in, a shrill alarm fills the cabin. Program alarm twelve-oh-two.

133
00:12:01,825 --> 00:12:06,860
Neither astronaut knows what it means. Armstrong's heartbeat starts to climb.

134
00:12:07,999 --> 00:12:16,045
In Houston, a twenty six year old guidance officer named Steve Bales has seconds to decide whether to abort the landing of the century.

135
00:12:17,166 --> 00:12:19,804
Bales remembers a simulation from weeks earlier.

136
00:12:19,804 --> 00:12:25,078
The alarm means the computer is overloaded but still flying. He makes the call.

137
00:12:25,078 --> 00:12:26,962
We're go on that alarm.

138
00:12:28,099 --> 00:12:30,825
The alarms keep coming. Twelve-oh-two. Twelve-oh-one.

139
00:12:30,825 --> 00:12:36,731
Five of them in four minutes, each one a heartbeat from an abort.

140
00:12:36,731 --> 00:12:38,548
The computer keeps flying.

141
00:12:39,666 --> 00:12:43,103
Then Armstrong looks out the window and sees the next problem.

142
00:12:43,103 --> 00:12:47,790
The computer is flying them into a crater full of boulders the size of cars.

143
00:12:48,933 --> 00:12:57,664
He takes manual control, levels off, and starts flying the lunar module like a helicopter, skimming the surface, hunting for a clear patch.

144
00:12:57,664 --> 00:13:00,322
And burning fuel he does not have.

145
00:13:01,466 --> 00:13:09,329
In Houston, the fuel callouts start. Sixty seconds. The room goes silent. Thirty seconds.

146
00:13:09,329 --> 00:13:12,699
Nobody in mission control is breathing.

147
00:13:13,833 --> 00:13:15,426
Contact light. Engine stop.

148
00:13:15,426 --> 00:13:21,800
The Eagle has landed, with roughly twenty five seconds of landing fuel left in the tanks.

149
00:13:22,933 --> 00:13:25,657
Charlie Duke's answer from Houston says it all.

150
00:13:25,657 --> 00:13:31,785
Roger, Tranquility, we copy you on the ground. You got a bunch of guys about to turn blue.

151
00:13:31,785 --> 00:13:32,807
We're breathing again.

152
00:13:33,933 --> 00:13:34,854
They were down.

153
00:13:34,854 --> 00:13:40,685
And the moment the engine stopped, every mind in the program shifted to the only question that now mattered.

154
00:13:40,685 --> 00:13:42,527
Can we get them back up?

155
00:13:43,666 --> 00:13:46,827
Chapter seven. Magnificent desolation.

156
00:13:47,966 --> 00:13:51,630
Before the famous steps, there was a quiet moment nobody saw.

157
00:13:51,630 --> 00:13:57,292
Buzz Aldrin pulled out a small kit from his personal allowance, and took communion on the Moon.

158
00:13:58,433 --> 00:14:04,139
Bread and wine, in one sixth gravity, the wine curling slowly up the side of the cup.

159
00:14:04,139 --> 00:14:06,152
NASA kept it off the broadcast.

160
00:14:06,152 --> 00:14:09,509
They were being sued over religious broadcasts from Apollo 8.

161
00:14:10,633 --> 00:14:19,479
Then the hatch opened, and for two and a half hours the two men did what six hundred million people will never forget.

162
00:14:19,479 --> 00:14:21,787
The steps. The words. The flag.

163
00:14:22,933 --> 00:14:28,575
Aldrin described the landscape in two words that became immortal. Magnificent desolation.

164
00:14:29,699 --> 00:14:36,721
Nixon called them from the Oval Office, live, calling it the most historic telephone call ever made from the White House.

165
00:14:36,721 --> 00:14:38,058
He kept it short.

166
00:14:39,199 --> 00:14:44,843
What almost nobody watching knew: the man on the phone had the other speech in a folder.

167
00:14:44,843 --> 00:14:47,166
The one that began, fate has ordained.

168
00:14:48,299 --> 00:14:51,010
The moonwalk ended. The samples were sealed.

169
00:14:51,010 --> 00:14:59,140
The two men climbed back into the Eagle, repressurized the cabin, and started preparing for the one moment everything depended on.

170
00:15:00,266 --> 00:15:06,457
And that is when Buzz Aldrin looked down at the floor, and saw something lying in the dust that should not have been there.

171
00:15:07,599 --> 00:15:10,446
Chapter eight. The broken switch.

172
00:15:11,566 --> 00:15:13,779
It was a small black plastic knob.

173
00:15:13,779 --> 00:15:20,735
Aldrin picked it up and started scanning the instrument panels, row by row, looking for the socket it had snapped off from.

174
00:15:21,866 --> 00:15:24,339
He found it. And his heart sank.

175
00:15:24,339 --> 00:15:29,990
Of all the hundreds of breakers in the cabin, the broken one was labeled ENGINE ARM.

176
00:15:31,133 --> 00:15:37,832
The engine arm circuit breaker closed the electrical path that armed the ascent engine.

177
00:15:37,832 --> 00:15:41,660
The one engine. The no-backup, one-attempt, get-off-the-Moon engine.

178
00:15:42,799 --> 00:15:48,675
In the cramped cabin, one of their bulky life support backpacks had almost certainly knocked it off.

179
00:15:48,675 --> 00:15:51,785
The cabin was barely bigger than two phone booths.

180
00:15:52,933 --> 00:15:56,597
Without that circuit closed, the ascent engine could not be armed.

181
00:15:56,597 --> 00:16:02,259
Right there, lying on the floor in the moon dust, was the beginning of the Safire memo.

182
00:16:03,399 --> 00:16:08,177
They told Houston, and Houston told them to sleep while the ground worked the problem.

183
00:16:08,177 --> 00:16:13,273
Imagine that night. Trying to sleep in a machine that may have just become your tomb.

184
00:16:14,399 --> 00:16:20,124
Aldrin later wrote that he slept on the floor, cold, with the broken breaker above him.

185
00:16:20,124 --> 00:16:26,206
Armstrong slept on the engine cover, under a rigged hammock, staring at the Earth through the window.

186
00:16:27,333 --> 00:16:31,542
On the ground, engineers debated fixes. Could they route around it?

187
00:16:31,542 --> 00:16:34,220
Could a metal pen bridge the contact?

188
00:16:34,220 --> 00:16:40,342
Metal risked a short circuit, and a short circuit risked far worse than a dead engine.

189
00:16:41,466 --> 00:16:47,123
Aldrin looked at what he had. And in his kit was a felt-tip marker. Plastic body.

190
00:16:47,123 --> 00:16:52,072
No exposed metal. The tip was just about the width of the breaker slot.

191
00:16:53,199 --> 00:17:01,162
In his own words: I inserted the pen into the small opening where the circuit breaker switch should have been, and pushed it in.

192
00:17:01,162 --> 00:17:03,152
Sure enough, the circuit breaker held.

193
00:17:04,299 --> 00:17:07,235
The ascent engine was armed by a pen.

194
00:17:07,235 --> 00:17:12,005
Mankind's return ticket from another world, held closed by a piece of stationery.

195
00:17:13,133 --> 00:17:17,887
Aldrin kept that pen, and the broken breaker, for the rest of his life.

196
00:17:17,887 --> 00:17:23,660
He said, I use prosaic tools all the time, but none of them ever meant as much.

197
00:17:24,799 --> 00:17:27,490
Chapter nine. If the pen had failed.

198
00:17:28,633 --> 00:17:33,981
Play the other timeline for a moment, because NASA had played it many times.

199
00:17:33,981 --> 00:17:37,802
The countdown reaches zero, the valves open... and nothing happens.

200
00:17:38,933 --> 00:17:42,850
The lunar module's remaining consumables gave the crew roughly a day.

201
00:17:42,850 --> 00:17:49,617
Battery power and oxygen, ticking down in a cabin the size of a closet, with the whole world listening.

202
00:17:50,766 --> 00:17:53,364
There was no version where help arrived.

203
00:17:53,364 --> 00:17:56,704
The next lunar module was months from flight readiness.

204
00:17:56,704 --> 00:18:01,528
Collins was sixty miles up with no way down. The physics was final.

205
00:18:02,666 --> 00:18:10,269
Houston would have tried everything anyway — every reroute, every jumper, every procedure, until the last hour.

206
00:18:10,269 --> 00:18:13,846
That was mission control's religion. Work the problem.

207
00:18:14,966 --> 00:18:19,753
And when the options ran out, the Safire protocol would have begun.

208
00:18:19,753 --> 00:18:25,337
The phone calls to Janet Armstrong and Joan Aldrin. The speech. Fate has ordained.

209
00:18:26,466 --> 00:18:31,557
Then the strangest mercy in the whole plan. NASA closing down communications.

210
00:18:31,557 --> 00:18:37,072
Not because the radios failed. So the world would not hear the end.

211
00:18:38,199 --> 00:18:44,267
What the two men would have done with their final hours is something Aldrin was once asked directly.

212
00:18:44,267 --> 00:18:48,648
His answer was unsentimental. We weren't going to spend it moaning about it.

213
00:18:49,766 --> 00:18:57,267
Some historians believe the crew might have chosen to end things on their own terms rather than wait for the air to thicken.

214
00:18:57,267 --> 00:19:00,528
The astronauts themselves rarely discussed it. Some doors stay closed.

215
00:19:01,666 --> 00:19:10,240
The Moon would carry two bodies in Sea of Tranquility, visible to every future mission, preserved unchanged for millions of years.

216
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:15,955
The memo's final line would be literal. Some corner of another world, forever mankind.

217
00:19:17,099 --> 00:19:21,723
That timeline died the moment a felt-tip pen clicked into a breaker slot.

218
00:19:21,723 --> 00:19:26,346
What happened instead was eighteen of the most relieving minutes in human history.

219
00:19:27,466 --> 00:19:29,817
Chapter ten. The ride home.

220
00:19:30,966 --> 00:19:34,876
July 21st. The countdown reaches zero.

221
00:19:34,876 --> 00:19:43,348
Aldrin says the words: nine, eight, seven, six, five... engine arm, ascent. Proceed.

222
00:19:44,466 --> 00:19:46,189
The engine lights. First try.

223
00:19:46,189 --> 00:19:55,150
The ascent stage leaps off the descent platform in a spray of gold foil and dust, and Aldrin watches the flag whip over in the exhaust.

224
00:19:56,299 --> 00:19:58,807
Eighteen minutes later they are in lunar orbit.

225
00:19:58,807 --> 00:20:05,390
A few hours after that, Collins watches the Eagle rise toward him and snaps a photograph of it against the Earth.

226
00:20:06,533 --> 00:20:08,733
That photo has a famous caption.

227
00:20:08,733 --> 00:20:14,232
Every human being alive or who had ever lived is in this picture, except one.

228
00:20:14,232 --> 00:20:16,799
Michael Collins, the man holding the camera.

229
00:20:17,933 --> 00:20:23,124
Three days later, Columbia hit the Pacific. The carrier Hornet pulled them out.

230
00:20:23,124 --> 00:20:29,113
Nixon stood grinning at the quarantine window — reading the speech history got to keep.

231
00:20:30,233 --> 00:20:34,472
In a filing cabinet in Washington, the other speech stayed in its folder.

232
00:20:34,472 --> 00:20:40,342
Two pages that came within one plastic switch of being the most famous words of the twentieth century.

233
00:20:41,466 --> 00:20:44,313
Chapter eleven. The speech that waits.

234
00:20:45,433 --> 00:20:52,536
The memo surfaced in the National Archives in 1999, thirty years late, and it changed how people saw Apollo.

235
00:20:52,536 --> 00:20:57,397
The triumph had been real. So had the coffin built next to it.

236
00:20:58,533 --> 00:21:01,104
Safire himself said it plainly, years later.

237
00:21:01,104 --> 00:21:06,246
If the mission had failed, the President would have had to console the nation.

238
00:21:06,246 --> 00:21:10,654
We had to be ready. Nobody wanted to be ready. We were.

239
00:21:11,799 --> 00:21:15,754
In 2020, MIT researchers used the memo for something unsettling.

240
00:21:15,754 --> 00:21:23,267
They built a deepfake of Nixon actually delivering the moon disaster speech, to teach people how synthetic media lies.

241
00:21:24,399 --> 00:21:30,586
Watching it is vertigo. The alternate timeline, staring back at you in broadcast gray.

242
00:21:30,586 --> 00:21:35,005
The saddest speech never given, given anyway, by a machine.

243
00:21:36,133 --> 00:21:39,219
Here is what this story is really about.

244
00:21:39,219 --> 00:21:46,164
Apollo 11 succeeded not because failure was impossible, but because failure was planned for in exact, unflinching detail.

245
00:21:47,299 --> 00:21:54,015
The people who wrote death protocols and the people who armed engines with felt-tip pens were the same organization.

246
00:21:54,015 --> 00:21:58,610
Hope for everything. Prepare for the worst thing you can say out loud.

247
00:21:59,733 --> 00:22:02,018
Every mission since carries that lesson.

248
00:22:02,018 --> 00:22:08,493
And somewhere in a drawer, there is always a version of the speech. For Artemis. For Mars.

249
00:22:08,493 --> 00:22:11,540
Written in advance, hoped never to be read.

250
00:22:12,666 --> 00:22:15,273
Because that is the honest price of exploration.

251
00:22:15,273 --> 00:22:19,510
Somebody has to write the words for the day the engine doesn't light.

252
00:22:20,633 --> 00:22:28,525
If this story gripped you, watch our full documentary on every disaster in spaceflight history — the missions where the speech did get read.

253
00:22:28,525 --> 00:22:31,813
And subscribe to mindwired for a new story every week.

